WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.125
Each sphere starts from a base state.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.694
But sphere one drags fluid past sphere two.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.505
Subtract that flow, W, from the base.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.947
Sphere two does the same to one.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.644
So each state depends on the other: a loop.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.490
By symmetry, both states are equal.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.123
Keep substituting: each hop is t times shorter.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.377
The sum: one over one plus t.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.288
Twelve spheres: stack every state into q.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.588
Row i sums its neighbors' flow.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.619
Call the right side T.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.435
Start from the base state; apply T repeatedly.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.761
Each pass adds another power of A; here, the terms shrink.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.650
With ninety-six crowded spheres, the terms grow.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.468
The largest eigenvalue size of A exceeds one.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.370
One equation for all states.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.525
Substitution only converges with shrinking powers of A.
